Usage: perry-okr <subcommand> [flags]
  build            derive the store from OKR.md and print it
  verify           field-compare the store on disk with the file
  write            the one-way import: OKR.md -> okr.jsonl
  render           okr.jsonl -> OKR.md; refuses when a record has no line to land in
  diff             render and byte-compare with the file on disk
  migrate-ids      mint O-<n> into the objective records of okr.jsonl that have none, and fill every KR's objective_id

  perry-okr <subcommand> --help   the flags for one of them
  perry-okr --describe --json     the whole surface as data

`OKR.md` as a store, and the projection of it — ADR-007's second slice.

    perry-okr build   [--root <p>]              derive the store; write nothing
    perry-okr verify  [--root <p>]              field-compare the store to the file
    perry-okr render  [--root <p>] [--write]    the store → OKR.md
    perry-okr write   [--root <p>] --from-file  OKR.md → the store
    perry-okr diff    [--root <p>]              render and byte-compare
                                                (exit 3: the bytes match and
                                                 the store did not produce them)
    perry-okr migrate-ids [--root <p>]          mint the Objective ids into
                                                okr.jsonl (DESIGN-009 step 3)

The store is `okr.jsonl`, beside `OKR.md` in the state root. It holds what is
WRITTEN — every KR (from a KR table or from the legacy `- KR1: …` bullet form),
every `## Commitments` row, every `## Versioning log` row — and nothing else.
The mission, the operating principles and every rationale paragraph are
LAYOUT: never parsed, never re-rendered, and reproduced byte for byte.

The record shape, the scanner and the renderer live in `bin/perry_md_store.py`,
and the CELL MODEL under them lives in `bin/perry_store.py` — the same one
`BOARD.md` is rendered through. Three tools, one model: a second cell model is
the defect ADR-007 exists to remove, and it would first show up on a rule like
"a declared blank marker is layout, not data" coming to mean two different
things in two files.

`bin/perry-goals` is the LANE tool and owns every write to this file's content.
This one is the store's migration and inspection surface, the way
`bin/perry-tasks` is for `bin/perry-task`.
